Leave the JIT region executable between translation blocks.

On Apple silicon a JIT page is either writable or executable, never both, and
the switch goes through pthread_jit_write_protect_np. Unicorn's cpu_tb_exec()
locks the region before running a block and unlocks it straight afterwards, so
every block costs a pair of those calls. Profiling the 5012H emulator, that
pair was a third of the whole runtime.

The unlock is not needed. Each path that writes into the region - tb_gen_code,
do_tb_phys_invalidate, tb_add_jump, uc_tb_flush - already brackets itself with
tb_exec_unlock/tb_exec_lock, so leaving the region executable between blocks
takes nothing away from them. The lock BEFORE the block does have to stay:
those writers leave the region writable on the way out and rely on the next
block to put it back. Removing that one instead is an immediate SIGBUS.

Measured on unicorn 2.1.4, macOS 26.5, M-series: 1.20-1.30x on the emulator's
own workload, and the six checked-in screenshots come out byte-identical, so
nothing about what is emulated changes.

index 77168f3..8153746 100644
--- a/qemu/accel/tcg/cpu-exec.c
+++ b/qemu/accel/tcg/cpu-exec.c
@@ -58,10 +58,9 @@ static inline tcg_target_ulong cpu_tb_exec(CPUState *cpu, TranslationBlock *itb)
     UC_TRACE_START(UC_TRACE_TB_EXEC);
     tb_exec_lock(cpu->uc);
     ret = tcg_qemu_tb_exec(env, tb_ptr);
-    if (cpu->uc->nested_level == 1) {
-        // Only unlock (allow writing to JIT area) if we are the outmost uc_emu_start
-        tb_exec_unlock(cpu->uc);
-    }
+    /* Left executable on purpose: every path that writes code unlocks for
+     * itself, so unlocking here only bought a pthread_jit_write_protect_np
+     * pair per translation block. See no-per-block-jit-toggle.patch. */
     UC_TRACE_END(UC_TRACE_TB_EXEC, "[uc] exec tb 0x%" PRIx64 ": ", itb->pc);
 
     cpu->can_do_io = 1;
